# Run Gradle from the correct build directory

This repo is **two independent Gradle builds** with their own wrappers pinned to
different Gradle versions (ADR-0015). Running `./gradlew` from the wrong directory
silently uses the wrong toolchain and fails or misbehaves.

| Build | Run from | Wrapper | Modules |
|-------|----------|---------|---------|
| SDK build | repo root | Gradle 8.11.1 (`./gradlew`) | `protocol`, `sdk-android`, samples, plugin |
| studio build | `studio/` | Gradle 9.3.1 (`studio/gradlew`) | `engine`, `host`, `daemon`, `shared`, `desktopApp`, `cli`, `mcp` |

## Rules

- **Chain an absolute `cd` inside the command; do NOT rely on the Shell `working_directory`
  parameter alone** — in this environment it is not reliably applied, so `./gradlew` silently
  runs from the repo root (8.11.1) even when `working_directory` says `studio`. Always write
  `cd /abs/path/to/repo/studio && ./gradlew …`.
- To build/test/run **`engine`, `host`, `daemon`, `shared`, `desktopApp`, `cli`, or `mcp`**, run studio's wrapper. NEVER run
  these from the repo root — root's `./gradlew` is 8.11.1 and does not know these modules.
- `protocol` is the seam: `:protocol:publishToMavenLocal` is a **root** task and must be run
  (from repo root) before the studio build, which consumes `wailo-protocol` from Maven Local.

```bash
# studio (engine/host/daemon/shared/desktopApp/cli/mcp) — absolute cd, then studio's ./gradlew (9.3.1)
cd /abs/path/to/repo/studio && ./gradlew :shared:jvmTest
cd /abs/path/to/repo/studio && ./gradlew :desktopApp:run

# SDK build (protocol/sdk-android/samples/plugin) — from repo root (8.11.1)
cd /abs/path/to/repo && ./gradlew :protocol:publishToMavenLocal
cd /abs/path/to/repo && ./gradlew :sample-android:assembleDebug
```

## Studio dev loop: check `hotRun` before you compile

The studio desktop app is usually already running under **Compose Hot Reload auto mode**
(`cd studio && ./gradlew :desktopApp:hotRun --auto`) in one of the user's terminals. Auto mode
**continuously recompiles and hot-reloads on every source edit**, so a manual studio build is
redundant and slow — and a second sandboxed `./gradlew` can even fail on stale caches.

**Before running any studio compile/build/run, check whether `hotRun` is already live:**

- Look in the terminals folder for a terminal whose `active_command` contains
  `:desktopApp:hotRun` (its output shows `Compose Hot Reload` and `> :desktopApp:hotRun` / `> IDLE`).
- **If it IS running:** do NOT run `:desktopApp:run` or a manual `compileKotlin`/`build`. Just
  edit the source — hot reload rebuilds automatically. Read that terminal's file to confirm the
  reload and to surface any compile errors.
- **If it is NOT running:** build/run/compile normally (above), or start it with
  `cd studio && ./gradlew :desktopApp:hotRun --auto`.

`hotRun` only covers the running app — tests (e.g. `:shared:jvmTest`) are a separate task and
still need their own invocation.

## Kill the daemon before reinstalling studio dists

A running daemon was launched with `-cp .../cli/build/install/wailo-cli/lib/*.jar` and loads classes
lazily, so `:cli:installDist` / `:daemon:installDist` while it is alive swaps the jar out from under it.
It keeps answering `ping` — so frontends happily attach — and then dies on `NoClassDefFoundError` for
every class it had not loaded yet, which now surfaces as
`Wailo daemon did not become ready (it answered: NoClassDefFoundError: …)`. Studio and the CLI both fail
that way until the process is gone; SIGTERM may not be enough, since its shutdown hook can die the same
way. Before reinstalling: `pkill -f com.venbiasa.wailo.daemon.MainKt` (add `-9` if it lingers).

Better still, keep end-to-end runs off the user's live daemon entirely — there is one daemon per state
directory, so point `WAILO_HOME` (plus `WAILO_CAPTURE_PORT`) at a scratch dir and the run gets its own.

## Agent shell is sandboxed: don't verify studio changes with `./gradlew`

The agent's Shell tool runs inside a **sandbox** that breaks the Gradle wrapper in ways the user's own
terminal never sees. Do not read a failed agent-side `./gradlew` as a real build failure without first
ruling these out — two distinct failure modes, both observed:

- **Default (sandboxed) run:** the wrapper's `xargs` call hits a blocked syscall
  (`xargs: sysconf(_SC_ARG_MAX) failed`), which derails the script so it can't find the installed Gradle
  and instead tries to **download** it from `services.gradle.org` — blocked by the network allowlist
  (`UnknownHostException`). It fails in <1s and *looks* like Gradle is missing, even though the pinned
  version is already installed and the user's own terminal builds the same target in seconds.
- **Sandbox disabled (`required_permissions: ["all"]`):** no download, but it spins up a *separate, cold*
  Gradle/Kotlin daemon (full Compose-compiler warmup — minutes) that also contends with the user's warm
  `hotRun` daemon. Slow, not broken — easy to mistake for a hang and interrupt.

**So verify studio (`shared`/`engine`/`desktopApp`) edits WITHOUT running `studio/gradlew` yourself:**

1. `ReadLints` on the edited files — catches unresolved references, missing imports, and type errors
   (this alone would have caught the one real bug in the top-bar-height change: a missing `height` import).
2. Read the running `hotRun --auto` terminal file — it recompiles on save and prints
   `e: <file>:<line> …` compile errors there (the fastest real-compiler signal available to the agent).
3. Only if a genuine build is unavoidable: ask the user to run it in their warm, unsandboxed terminal,
   or run with `required_permissions: ["all"]` and expect a slow cold compile — don't keep retrying the
   default sandboxed path, it will just keep hitting the `xargs`/download failure above.

The studio build needs `wailo-protocol` in local Maven, so build it locally from `studio/`.
